.env
.env.*
!.env.example
MASTER_CONTEXT.md
.claude/skills/
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/settings.local.json
.cursor/skills/
# The Codex mirror, and the ONE line here that must not carry a trailing slash:
# it is a symlink, which git matches as a file, so `.agents/skills/` ignores
# nothing and the link lands in `git status` on every clone. Measured, not read.
.agents/skills
.DS_Store
*.log

# Generation logs are the USER'S session history, not repo content. The file is
# created on first run and stays local: a committed log ships real creditsCharged
# values, which is a de-facto price table — exactly what this repo refuses to hold.
# logs/README.md is the schema and stays tracked.
logs/*.jsonl
# ...and the twin the documented update recipe leaves behind. logs/README.md
# tells you to rewrite the log as `"$LOG" > "$LOG.tmp" && mv "$LOG.tmp" "$LOG"`,
# precisely so an interrupted update cannot truncate it. That makes the leftover
# the EXPECTED outcome of a failure, and it is a byte-for-byte copy of every
# creditsCharged and job id the line above exists to keep local.
logs/*.jsonl.tmp
__pycache__/
*.pyc
outputs/
output/

# caption-video's DOCUMENTED output layout: it writes
# `<source-video>-with-captions.mp4` and a `<run-id>-captions/` project
# (source.mp4, trimmed.mp4, transcripts, renders/*.mov) BESIDE the file it was
# handed, deliberately not under a fixed parent, so no existing home covered it.
# What lands there is a customer's raw and rendered footage, in a clone whose
# next commit is a public PR.
#
# Two things about the shape of these two lines, both load-bearing:
#
# They sit ABOVE the `references/` re-includes below because .gitignore is
# LAST-MATCH-WINS. Placed after them, an unanchored `*-captions/` re-excludes
# `shared/skills/caption-video/references/style-captions/`, a path those very
# lines exist to rescue, and the negation silently loses.
#
# They are ROOT-ANCHORED because a pattern with no leading slash matches at EVERY
# depth: bare `*-captions/` swallows a future `skills/auto-captions/` whole,
# SKILL.md and all, so check-skill-frontmatter.sh would never see the skill; bare
# `*-with-captions.mp4` swallows a gallery asset like
# `assets/gallery/demo-with-captions.mp4`. Anchoring costs nothing real: run in a
# fresh clone the skill's output IS at the root, and every other sanctioned home
# it could run inside (outputs/ above, references/ and generated/ and
# iterations/ below) is already ignored in full.
/*-captions/
/*-with-captions.mp4

# All reference media is local-only (face photos, product shots, style boards).
# Drop your refs into references/influencers/, references/products/,
# references/aesthetics/, etc. — they stay on your machine.
references/

# ...except a skill's OWN reference DOCS, which are part of the skill and must
# ship with it. `references/` above is about media a user supplies; a file like
# skills/pixar-ad/references/formulas.md is documentation the SKILL.md
# links to, and silently excluding it publishes a skill with a dead link.
# (Git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, hence both
# lines: the directory first, then its contents.)
!skills/*/references/
!skills/*/references/**

# ...and the same for shared/references/, which is docs too: craft.md and the
# README describing the media folders above. `references/` matches a directory of
# that name at ANY depth, so without these two lines a new file here is swallowed
# silently — `shared/references/README.md` only survives because it was tracked
# before the rule existed, and git ignores nothing it already tracks. That is the
# trap: the directory looks fine in `git status` right up until you add to it.
!shared/references/
!shared/references/**

# ...and the same again for a SHARED skill's own reference docs. The rule two
# blocks up re-includes `skills/*/references/`, which does not reach
# `shared/skills/*/references/`: four shared skills carry a SKILL.md, so a
# reference doc added under one of them is dropped by `git add -A` while it sits
# there on disk. check-links.sh then fails on BOTH sides, and says so precisely:
# it asks `git ls-files --error-unmatch`, not the filesystem, so the local run
# already reports "exists locally but is NOT COMMITTED" rather than passing and
# leaving CI to find it.
!shared/skills/*/references/
!shared/skills/*/references/**


# Disposable one-off thumbnail iteration scripts (not the canonical skill script)
scripts/generate-thumbnails.sh
scripts/generate-thumbnails-*.sh
scripts/generate-ab-variants*.sh
scripts/regen-*.sh

# Generated ad renders are the user's output, not repo content (the script's
# default --out is ./generated). Same reasoning as logs/*.jsonl and references/.
generated/

# Prompt files a session composes. A prompt long enough to matter does not survive
# being typed into a shell argument, and the 4,000-character cap forces several
# rewrites before one passes — so agents reach for files here without being told.
# Demonstrably: on 2026-08-08 a run building image ads created `prompts/` on its
# own, wrote ten .txt files into it, and handed the user a "+162 / Create PR" badge
# over work they never asked to commit. The directory was the right instinct; the
# missing line here was the bug. Same reasoning as generated/ — session
# work-product, not repo content.
prompts/

# Clone rounds: `iterations/clone-<date>/<tag>/prompt.txt`, `v1.png`, … This is the
# clone-image-ad skill's DOCUMENTED output dir, so following that skill exactly
# dirtied the tree the same way — the renders are the user's evidence of a template,
# not repo content. `git add -f iterations/<path>` still commits the rare one worth
# keeping.
iterations/

# Update-system runtime state, written by ./scripts/update.sh and the
# SessionStart hooks: the mkdir-lock, the out-of-band `.env` copy taken around a
# merge, the kill-switch config, the throttle stamps and the migration
# touchfiles. It is per-clone state, never repo content — and it must be IGNORED
# rather than merely untracked, because `git stash --include-untracked` sweeps
# untracked files: an unignored state dir would be stashed mid-update, taking
# the lock and the .env backup with it at the exact moment they are load-bearing.
# (update.sh also drops a self-ignoring `*` .gitignore inside it, so a clone
# whose copy of this file predates the line is covered too.)
.update-state/

# The documented home for skills you write or fork for yourself. Anything here
# is yours: it is never updated, never overwritten, and never shows up as a diff
# you did not make. Copy a shipped skill into it before editing, and the update
# path stops being a negotiation with your own edits.
local-skills/
